WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.833
A customer writes to support: charged twice for one bike rental.

2
00:00:06.833 --> 00:00:11.417
Support wants past complaints like it, by meaning, not by shared words.

3
00:00:11.600 --> 00:00:16.800
So duplicate charge, scooter hire should match, though it shares no word with the first.

4
00:00:16.800 --> 00:00:21.704
How does a database find complaints that mean the same thing, among millions, fast?

5
00:00:21.967 --> 00:00:26.047
First, meaning has to become something a computer can measure.

6
00:00:26.047 --> 00:00:34.801
A model, a program trained on huge amounts of text, turns each text into a vector: a list of a few hundred to a few thousand numbers.

7
00:00:35.000 --> 00:00:39.289
Texts with similar meanings land close together, like points on a map.

8
00:00:39.289 --> 00:00:42.721
Only this map has hundreds of directions instead of two.

9
00:00:42.721 --> 00:00:47.500
So finding similar complaints means finding the nearest points to the new one.

10
00:00:47.667 --> 00:00:54.829
The plain way: measure the distance from the query to every vector, and keep the closest.

11
00:00:54.829 --> 00:01:01.589
In the lab, that's 100,000 vectors of 32 numbers, 2,138 pages, about 8 milliseconds.

12
00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:11.053
Now picture 50 million complaints of 1,536 numbers each: 77 billion multiplications and additions for every question.

13
00:01:11.053 --> 00:01:13.425
And a sorted index can't help.

14
00:01:13.425 --> 00:01:18.012
It has one order, and this map has hundreds of directions.

15
00:01:18.233 --> 00:01:21.997
So think how you drive to a friend's house in another city.

16
00:01:21.997 --> 00:01:26.398
Motorway first, with exits far apart, to the exit nearest their town.

17
00:01:26.398 --> 00:01:27.482
Then a main road.

18
00:01:27.482 --> 00:01:29.396
Then the streets, to the door.

19
00:01:29.533 --> 00:01:29.987
Map it.

20
00:01:29.987 --> 00:01:34.719
Every vector is a house on the streets, linked to its nearest neighbours.

21
00:01:34.719 --> 00:01:41.265
A random few are also on main roads, and fewer still on the motorway, with longer links between them.

22
00:01:41.500 --> 00:01:48.952
Here's a small one, drawn in one direction so it fits: 16 vectors, three layers, and the query, the yellow dot.

23
00:01:49.167 --> 00:01:53.538
The search starts at the single entry point on the motorway.

24
00:01:53.538 --> 00:01:59.074
It measures the distance to each neighbour, and moves to any that is closer.

25
00:01:59.074 --> 00:02:00.531
330 is closer: move.

26
00:02:00.531 --> 00:02:02.207
Then 570, closer still.

27
00:02:02.400 --> 00:02:06.197
From 570, no neighbour on this layer is closer.

28
00:02:06.197 --> 00:02:09.993
So it drops to the same vector, one layer down.

29
00:02:09.993 --> 00:02:12.174
There, 500 is closer: move.

30
00:02:12.174 --> 00:02:14.032
Nothing next to 500 is.

31
00:02:14.167 --> 00:02:20.756
On the streets, it stops taking the first better step, and keeps a beam: the 4 best vectors seen so far.

32
00:02:20.756 --> 00:02:25.508
It explores around them until nothing left can beat the worst one it holds.

33
00:02:25.833 --> 00:02:34.608
The beam is the answer: the four closest vectors, found with 10 distance calculations, where measuring every vector takes 16.

34
00:02:34.867 --> 00:02:42.937
This is called HNSW, hierarchical navigable small world, from a 2016 paper by Malkov and Yashunin.

35
00:02:42.937 --> 00:02:52.736
pgvector, an add-on that stores vectors in PostgreSQL, builds it, with 16 links per vector and a beam of 40 by default.

36
00:02:52.933 --> 00:03:02.988
In the lab, the walk read 654 pages, where measuring every vector read 2,138, and it answered in under a millisecond.

37
00:03:03.267 --> 00:03:06.147
That's great, but there's a catch in the word walk.

38
00:03:06.147 --> 00:03:08.292
The search only goes where links lead.

39
00:03:08.292 --> 00:03:11.116
A true neighbour behind a poor link can be missed.

40
00:03:11.333 --> 00:03:15.964
The share of the true nearest neighbours that comes back is called recall.

41
00:03:15.964 --> 00:03:20.720
In the lab, with a beam of 10, the walk found 89 percent of the true top 10.

42
00:03:20.720 --> 00:03:23.285
With the default beam of 40, all of them.

43
00:03:23.285 --> 00:03:26.101
A bigger beam finds more, and does more work.

44
00:03:26.300 --> 00:03:27.129
Now a trap.

45
00:03:27.129 --> 00:03:33.310
Support adds a filter that keeps about 1 complaint in 11, and asks for 10 results.

46
00:03:33.310 --> 00:03:34.742
How many come back?

47
00:03:34.742 --> 00:03:35.948
Pause and guess.

48
00:03:39.200 --> 00:03:40.201
4, on average.

49
00:03:40.201 --> 00:03:43.275
pgvector applies the filter after the walk.

50
00:03:43.275 --> 00:03:47.994
The walk collected its 40 candidates, and the filter kept about 4.

51
00:03:47.994 --> 00:03:50.640
Nothing fails, and nothing warns you.

52
00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:58.356
It's a sat-nav that finds 40 shops near you, throws away the closed ones, and proudly hands you 4.

53
00:03:58.356 --> 00:04:05.064
Since pgvector 0.8.0, an iterative scan keeps walking until enough rows pass: 10 of 10.

54
00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:09.869
And a cost: the index took 41 MB for 17 MB of vectors.

55
00:04:09.869 --> 00:04:16.215
Every hop jumps to a random place, so HNSW wants its whole graph in memory.

56
00:04:16.467 --> 00:04:25.839
So the moral of the story: to find nearest by meaning, walk the graph, big jumps on top and small steps at the bottom, and keep a short list of the best.

57
00:04:25.839 --> 00:04:29.515
You trade a little recall for a lot of speed, so measure it.

58
00:04:29.733 --> 00:04:30.892
One quick check.

59
00:04:30.892 --> 00:04:34.513
Your search returns 89 percent of the true top 10.

60
00:04:34.513 --> 00:04:35.961
What do you turn up?

61
00:04:35.961 --> 00:04:37.120
Pause and think.

62
00:04:40.333 --> 00:04:41.834
The beam, ef_search.

63
00:04:41.834 --> 00:04:47.387
In the lab, raising it from 10 to 40 found all 10, for a little more work.
